Ladybird’s Inspect Element item selects the element that was under the
cursor when the context menu opened, or the nearest element around a
text node there. WebKit does the same: ContextMenuController hands
InspectorController::inspect() the node from the menu’s own hit-test
result, and InspectorDOMAgent::inspect() steps from a text node up to
its parent. Gecko also takes the target of the menu when it opens. Blink
instead hit-tests when the item is chosen: Its WebDevToolsAgentImpl::
InspectElement() hit-tests at the point where the menu opened, and
InspectorOverlayAgent::Inspect() walks up to an element.

Ladybird hit-tests only the process of the top-level page, so in a
cross-site iframe it selects the iframe element. Blink hit-tests in the
frame the menu came from. And all three engines show the item whenever
their developer tools are available, opening them as needed, while
Ladybird shows it only while a DevTools client is inspecting the tab.
